Trace:

  1. convertToAssets only takes the escrow branch when t_CurrentAccount != address(0) AND
  _isWithdrawRequestPending(t_CurrentAccount), and even then only when the manager returns hasRequest == true; otherwise
  it falls through to super.convertToAssets(shares). The pricing decision is entirely delegated to
  withdrawRequestManager.getWithdrawRequestValue(vault, account, asset, shares), whose body is not in the file under
  review. The claim ("forwards an unclamped shares that no longer matches w.sharesAmount") is a claim about that unshown
  function's math, not about anything in this contract. I cannot point to a line here that mis-scales anything.
  2. The "partially escrowed" precondition is not reachable from this contract. _initiateWithdraw calls
  withdrawRequestManager.initiateWithdraw({... sharesAmount: sharesHeld ...}) — it registers the account's entire share
  balance against the request, not a subset. So a pending request covers all of the account's shares by construction;
  there is no leftover vault-held PT remainder for the same account alongside a pending request.
  3. Liquidation path: _postLiquidation calls tokenizeWithdrawRequest(liquidateAccount, liquidator, sharesToLiquidator).
  Tokenization carves out the liquidator's slice by splitting the request pro-rata — both yieldTokenAmount and
  sharesAmount are reduced together, which is exactly what preserves the yieldTokenAmount/sharesAmount ratio. Linear
  extrapolation from a "shrunken escrow claim" is therefore correct, not richer: the claim shrank in the same proportion
  as the shares. Nothing in the shown code reduces one without the other.
  4. Partial redemption path: _redeemShares(isEscrowed=true) computes yieldTokensBurned = w.yieldTokenAmount *
  sharesToRedeem / w.sharesAmount and passes both withdrawYieldTokenAmount and sharesToBurn into
  finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest. Again both legs of the ratio are decremented proportionally, so post-redemption the
  surviving request still satisfies yieldTokenAmount/sharesAmount ≈ the original ratio. The redeemed shares are burned
  from the account, so the caller's subsequent shares argument shrinks in lockstep. It also revert
  WithdrawRequestNotFinalized unless finalized, so an unfinalized request cannot be partially drained here at all.
  5. The alternative harm ("discarding the still-vault-held PT remainder → reads poorer") requires an account holding
  both escrowed and non-escrowed yield tokens simultaneously; per (2) initiateWithdraw escrows against sharesHeld (full
  balance), so that split state has no constructor in this code.

  So: the exploit's precondition (shares argument desynchronized from w.sharesAmount while both legs of the escrow claim
  are not proportionally adjusted) is contradicted by the two proportional-split call sites actually present, and the
  arithmetic the finding accuses is in an unshown contract. Under the tie-break, uncertainty about
  getWithdrawRequestValue's internals kills the claim rather than saving it.

